      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-315235",
      ":question/problem": "In a lab experiment, the temperature of a chemical reactor (in ${}^{\u2218}C$) after $t$ minutes is given by the function $T(t).$ The values of $T(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.2$ | $1.6$ | $2.0$ | $2.4$ | $2.8$ | $3.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$T(t)$** | $28$ | $30$ | $32$ | $34$ | $35$ | $36$ |\n\nApproximate the reactor\u2019s temperature instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.0$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.4.$\n\nThe reactor\u2019s temperature at $t=2.0$ is changing at approximately \n{{field-1}} ${}^{\u2218}C$ per minute."
